{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_66","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"Release EX on RV32M result cycle\n\nWhat changed:\n- Refined `ex_stage.sv` so the RV32M handshake distinguishes accepting a new M op, waiting for the sequential unit, and emitting a completed result.\n- `busy` now deasserts on an unstalled `muldiv_done` cycle, letting hazard/decode accept the next instruction while EX/MEM captures the saved M payload and result.\n- Added a small pending-result latch so a result is not lost if `muldiv_done` coincides with an EX/MEM stall.\n- Updated `hazard_unit.sv` comments to document that `ex_busy` means \"not ready for younger EX work\" and is low on the released result cycle.\n- Added `test_muldiv_release.py` to check MUL/DIVU/REMU followed by independent and dependent instructions, including a `dmem_ready=0` run with no EX/MEM memory op so the result-cycle release is preserved.\n\nDeviations:\n- No top-level port or RVFI retirement changes were made.\n- The existing hazard interface was kept as a single `ex_busy` signal instead of adding a new ready port; its meaning was narrowed in EX.\n\nConcerns:\n- In this pipeline, a real dmem stall cannot normally overlap an in-flight M wait because EX/MEM is bubbled while the M unit runs. The pending-result latch still covers that interface case defensively.\n\nLocal checks:\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: pass.\n- `pytest -q cores/bench/test/test_muldiv_release.py`: pass.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`: pass, 53 passed / 0 failed.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`: pass, 2 ELFs tested. Plain `make cosim TARGET=bench` defaults the harness to `NRET=2` and fails to build against this `nret: 1` core, so the local rerun set `NRET=1` to match `core.yaml` and the orchestrator environment.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
