{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_122","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"RV32M held execute implementation notes\n\nChanged:\n- Removed real multiply/divide/remainder hardware from `alu.sv`; hardware builds now keep the ALU to the fast RV32I path, with only RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS stand-ins left for formal mode.\n- Added `muldiv_unit.sv` with registered multiply results and an iterative restoring divider/remainder sequencer. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, the unit returns the riscv-formal substitute results quickly.\n- Updated `ex_stage.sv` to capture M-op operands/RVFI payload, bubble EX/MEM while the unit runs, then emit the completed result through the existing MEM/WB retirement path.\n- Added an execute-busy stall source in `hazard_unit.sv` and wired it through `core.sv`. During M busy, fetch is held and ID/EX is bubbled so the younger instruction is decoded fresh after the M result is ready.\n- Added `00_core_pkg.sv` as a guarded source-order shim so the prompt's raw `rtl/*.sv` Verilator lint command sees `core_pkg.sv` definitions before `core.sv`.\n- Moved RV32M cocotb vector coverage from `test_alu.py` into new `test_muldiv.py`, and added a pipeline M hold/forward test.\n\nDeviations:\n- MEM/WB forwarding remains allowed when `mem_wb.valid` is clear, matching the existing dmem-stall design. The MEM/WB register intentionally preserves completed data during dmem waits while clearing `valid` to avoid double retirement.\n- The divider is 32-cycle iterative in real builds; only formal ALTOPS mode uses the short substitute path.\n\nConcerns:\n- CoreMark will pay the expected CPI cost for DIV/REM-heavy regions. MUL is also one held cycle rather than fully combinational.\n- The source-order shim is only for compatibility with raw shell glob lint; normal build scripts still force `core_pkg.sv` first.\n\nLocal status:\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: pass.\n- `make test TARGET=bench`: pass.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`: pass, 53 passed / 0 failed.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`: pass. Bare `make cosim TARGET=bench` defaults the C++ harness to `NRET=2` in this shell and fails to build against the core's `nret: 1` port set.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
