{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"Tiny return-address-stack predictor\n\nChanged:\n- Added return-prediction metadata to the ID/EX bundle.\n- Added a 4-entry circular return-address stack in ID. It predicts only\n  canonical returns: `jalr x0, x1, 0` and `jalr x0, x5, 0`.\n- Added decode-time return redirects into IF. EX redirects keep priority;\n  decode redirects flush queued sequential fetches while allowing the return\n  itself to enter ID/EX.\n- Added EX-stage RAS training. Resolved jumps writing x1 or x5 push `pc+4`;\n  resolved canonical returns pop when they reach EX.\n- Suppressed the normal JALR redirect when a carried return prediction matches\n  the resolved target, and repaired control flow on prediction mismatch or a\n  predicted-return misalignment trap.\n\nDeviations:\n- The RAS is implemented inside `id_stage.sv` rather than as a separate module\n  to keep the lookup local to decode and avoid a new frontend timing path.\n- Overflow overwrites the oldest entry using a tiny circular stack.\n\nConcerns:\n- A same-cycle pop followed by decode of another return can still use the old\n  top entry for that younger return. This is safe because EX repairs\n  mismatches, but it may miss a rare back-to-back-return opportunity.\n- The predictor can fetch from a stale return address if software uses x1/x5\n  unconventionally. Targets come from executed call PCs, and mismatch repair is\n  still architectural.\n\nLocal status:\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: pass.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`: pass, 53 passed / 0 failed.\n- Bare `make cosim TARGET=bench` invoked the harness with its default\n  `NRET=2` and failed before simulation because this core is `nret: 1`.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`: pass, 2 ELFs tested.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
