{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r10s0.yaml && git status --short\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-001-r10s0\ntitle: \"Implicit-PC IF FIFO\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current best core gets its IPC gain from the two-entry instruction fetch\n  queue, but the remaining worst paths repeatedly end in that queue's count or\n  queued-PC/payload registers after passing through EX redirect control. Prior\n  attempts that added predictors or rewrote the queue's validity scheme lost\n  timing, so the next promising micro-optimization is to keep the exact FIFO\n  depth and handshake while removing unnecessary state from the fragile IF\n  update cone.\nhypothesis: |\n  The IF FIFO only ever holds sequential instructions between redirects, and a\n  redirect already flushes the queue before fetching from the new target. That\n  means queued PCs can be derived from fetch_pc_q and count_q instead of being\n  stored and shifted as pc0_q/pc1_q payload. Replace the explicit queued-PC\n  registers with an implicit head-PC calculation: for count 1 the head PC is\n  fetch_pc_q - 4, for count 2 it is fetch_pc_q - 8, and the empty bypass still\n  uses fetch_pc_q directly.\n\n  Instruction payload storage, count transitions, imem push, decode pop, and\n  redirect flush semantics stay unchanged, so CoreMark's stall-hiding IPC\n  should match the champion. The expected win is Fmax: the redirect/decode-pop\n  feedback no longer drives wide queued-PC register muxes, and recent timing\n  reports often name pc0_q or nearby IF payload state as the endpoint.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Remove pc0_q/pc1_q from the two-entry fetch queue and derive the queued head PC from fetch_pc_q plus count_q while preserving existing push/pop/redirect behavior.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
