{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r11s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-001-r11s0\ntitle: \"Shared EX target adder\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion still times through the ID/EX operand state, EX redirect logic,\n  and the two-entry IF queue control. Prior predictor and IF-queue rewrites\n  repeatedly regressed because they added work to this same boundary. The\n  remaining promising micro-opt is to shorten the existing EX redirect\n  arithmetic without adding new state, branch latency, or front-end policy.\nhypothesis: |\n  Refactor ex_stage so PC-relative branch and JAL targets share one target\n  computation instead of building separate full-width branch_target and\n  jump_target adders. Because the core fetches aligned RV32I instructions and\n  B/J immediates have bit 0 fixed to zero, PC-relative target misalignment can\n  be detected from imm[1], and aligned redirect targets can be formed with a\n  30-bit word-address add plus 2'b00. Keep the full rs1+imm adder only for\n  JALR, whose bit 0 is architecturally cleared.\n\n  Also compute pc_plus4 once and reuse it for link writeback, fall-through\n  pc_next, and the held mul/div payload. This should remove duplicated\n  adders and low-bit target checks from the EX-to-IF redirect cone while\n  leaving IPC, RVFI pc_wdata, and misalignment trap behavior unchanged.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Share PC-relative target and pc+4 arithmetic, use a word-address adder for aligned branch/JAL redirects, and keep JALR on its existing full rs1+imm path.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Drop or narrow any EX/MEM branch-target bookkeeping that becomes redundant after the shared target calculation, while preserving RVFI-visible fields.\"\n  - file: test/te