{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git status --short cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r8s0.yaml && sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r8s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-001-r8s0\ntitle: \"Decode-latched M op flag\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current winning design already removed RV32M arithmetic from the normal ALU cone, but EX still re-decodes the 5-bit ALU opcode with an eight-way M-operation compare. That M detect feeds muldiv_start, busy, redirect gating, and the hazard unit's ex_busy path, so every branch/redirect and front-end hold decision still pays for a small but high-fanout comparator tree.\n\n  Recent attempts that add front-end prediction, store buffering, or broader source predecode have tended to regress because they lengthen fragile IF/redirect paths. A safer micro-optimization is to remove redundant EX control decoding without changing pipeline timing, memory behavior, or CoreMark IPC.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a one-bit is_m_op field to the decoded control bundle and set it only in the decoder's validated funct7=0000001 R-type M-extension arm. Latch that bit through ID/EX and use it directly in ex_stage for M-unit start/busy qualification instead of comparing alu_op against ALU_MUL through ALU_REMU.\n\n  This should shorten the EX busy/redirect/hazard cone and reduce fanout from the alu_op field while preserving the existing sequential muldiv unit, ALTOPS behavior, RVFI trap discipline, and nret=1 interface. Because it changes only redundant control decode, the expected IPC impact is neutral and any gain should come from Fmax.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add an is_m_op bit to ctrl_t so the decoder can carry validated M-extension classification into EX.\"\n  - file: rtl/decoder.sv\n    description: \"Assert the ne