{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,200p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r12s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-002-r12s1\ntitle: \"Two-phase write-first register file\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion's worst paths still originate around the operand source side and run through execute redirect into the small IF queue. Prior structural attempts that added predictor metadata, branch sidecars, wider ID/EX payloads, or extra memory ownership lost because they increased control fanout or IPC cost. A narrower structural target is the integer register file read path itself: every ID read currently carries x0 handling plus a write-first WB bypass compare and data mux on both read ports.\nhypothesis: |\n  Change the register-file topology so architectural writeback is committed in the first half of the cycle and decode reads the already-updated flop array in the second half, removing the same-cycle write-first bypass mux from the combinational read ports. Practically, implement the 32x32 integer file with a falling-edge write phase (or an equivalent local two-phase structure accepted by Gowin) while keeping x0 hardwired and preserving the visible pipeline timing: a value entering MEM/WB after a rising edge is written before the next rising edge where a dependent instruction's ID/EX operands are captured.\n\n  This should shorten the regfile-to-ID/EX/EX operand path without adding branch latency, fetch metadata, or new bypass sources. The new half-cycle MEM/WB-to-regfile write path is short, because it only contains the existing writeback mux and destination decode, while the more timing-sensitive read-to-redirect path loses a compare and 32-bit data mux per source operand. CoreMark IPC should be unchanged; the expected gain is Fmax recovery on the current operand/redirect critical cone.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \"Retopologize the integer register file into a two-phase write/read structure that preserves write-first architectural behavior without per-read WB bypass muxes.\"\n  - file: test/test_regfile_timing.py\n    description: \"Add focused cocotb coverage that a same-cycle WB producer is visible to a dependent decode operand, x0 remains hardwired, and reset/write ordering stays deterministic.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
