{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r1s0 (regression, +4.46%): Registering forwarding selects early can regress when EX still gates operand muxes with producer write-valid fallback logic; removing rd compares is not enough if the ALU input path gains deeper mux/control logic.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r1s1 (improvement, +752.39%): Moving RV32M into a held multi-cycle EX unit is a large win when it removes multiply/divide/remainder hardware from the common ALU-forwarding-branch-address cone, because CoreMark's infrequent M ops tolerate stalls better than every integer op tolerates the long combinational path.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 (regression, -0.58%): Static BTFN/JAL prediction in IF can lose when immediate decode plus a fetch-stage target adder lengthen the PC/imem path more than CoreMark's avoided taken-redirect bubbles improve IPC.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r2s0 (improvement, +24.85%): Splitting ID/EX bubbles so only valid/side-effecting control clears on redirect/load-use/imem/ex_busy cycles shortens the regfile-to-ID/EX cone because wide pc/rs/immediate/instr payload flops can capture freely while dmem stalls remain the only whole-bundle hold.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (regression, +21.59%): Retiming redirect squash into EX can regress when the saved EX-to-hazard flush arc is replaced by high-fanout `squash_id_ex`/`execute_valid` gating across branch/jump, RV32M start, trap, and EX/MEM side-effect controls.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r2s2 (regression, -26.61%): Even an EX-trained 8-entry BTB can lose when the fetch `pc` loop gains valid/tag/counter SRAM-style lookup, compare, and target mux delay; CoreMark's saved backedge bubbles did not repay the Fmax hit.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r3s0 (improvement, +5.78%): Giving loads/stores a dedicated EX/MEM `rs1+imm` address path helps when dmem masks, misalignment checks, and RVFI aligned addresses otherwise inherit the full generic `alu_result` shift/compare/jump mux delay.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 (regression, +0.36%): Spl\n…[7748 of 10820 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8f554b04fa7075c9]…\nX redirect for aligned JALs instead of carrying prediction repair bits through ID/EX or the hazard squash path.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r12s0 (regression, -9.06%): Short-cutting EX misalignment qualification through `imm[1]` and a 2-bit JALR adder can regress when the full target adders still feed redirect/RVFI data, because the new qualifier only duplicates high-fanout control on the same IF FIFO flush path.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r12s1 (regression, -2.77%): Replacing read-port write-first bypass compares with a negedge integer regfile write can regress when the half-cycle WB-to-flop path and falling-edge clocking cost more than the small local operand muxes they remove.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r12s2 (regression, -2.89%): Extending the metadata-free direct-JAL decode redirect to conditional branches regressed because `bp_pc`/`bp_instr` compares, redirect-target muxing, and an ID/EX `predicted_branch_taken` repair bit put predictor metadata back on the fragile decode-to-IF/EX control path.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
