{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r13s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-002-r13s1\ntitle: \"Tiny direct-mapped I-cache\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion has already harvested the low-risk control-flow wins,\n  and recent structural attempts that add redirect metadata or widen pipeline\n  control have regressed. The benchmark still drives about 22 percent random\n  instruction-memory stalls, and the two-entry IF FIFO only hides stalls that\n  happen after backend bubbles have prefilled it. CoreMark's hot loops should\n  revisit a compact instruction working set many times, so a tiny cache can\n  attack IPC without changing branch prediction or the architectural contract.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a small direct-mapped, one-word-per-line instruction cache inside the IF\n  stage, in front of the existing two-entry fetch queue. On a cache hit for the\n  current fetch PC, push the cached instruction into the queue even when\n  io_imemReady is low; on a miss, keep the current retry behavior and fill the\n  selected entry when io_imemReady returns. Redirects still flush only the IF\n  queue and restart fetch at the target, while cache contents remain valid\n  because the core has separate instruction and data memories and no\n  self-modifying-code requirement.\n\n  This should reduce the counted CoreMark cycles by hiding most repeated\n  instruction fetch stalls in hot loops. The design should be kept deliberately\n  small, for example 32 or 64 entries, so the hit check is only an indexed valid\n  bit, tag compare, and instruction mux feeding the existing queue. Even if the\n  added lookup costs a few percent of Fmax, the removed imem bubbles can exceed\n  the tournament replacement threshold.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add a tiny direct-mapped instruction cache that supplies repeated fetch PCs from local state and fills on io_imemReady misses while preserving redirect flush semantics.\"\n  - file: test/test_icache.py\n    description: \"Add focused cocotb coverage for cache hit-under-imem-stall, miss retry/fill, redirect queue flush with retained cache contents, and repeated-loop fetch behavior.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
