{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-003-r11s2\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r14s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r2s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-003-r8s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r3s1\\\") | \\\"--- \\\" + .id + \\\" \\\" + .title + \\\"\\\\n\\\" + .hypothesis.hypothesis + \\\"\\\\nlesson: \\\" + (.lesson//\\\"\\\") + \\\"\\\\nnotes: \\\" + (.implementation_notes//\\\"\\\")' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "--- hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 Retimed redirect squash\nRetiming control-flow squash should improve Fmax with little or no CoreMark\nIPC loss. Let EX redirect drive only the PC update in the redirect cycle, and\nallow the younger wrong-path instruction to be captured into ID/EX. A small\nregistered \"kill younger\" control in EX then converts that instruction into a\nbubble before it can update EX/MEM, dmem, regfile, or RVFI state. Load-use,\ndmem, imem, and mul/div busy stalls keep their existing replay behavior, but\nredirect no longer feeds hazard_unit flush_id/flush_if or the IF output NOP\nmux. This breaks the EX-to-hazard-to-ID feedback path while preserving the\nexisting branch recovery latency and the nret=1 RVFI contract.\n\nlesson: - 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (regression, +21.59%): Retiming redirect squash into EX can regress when the saved EX-to-hazard flush arc is replaced by high-fanout `squash_id_ex`/`execute_valid` gating across branch/jump, RV32M start, trap, and EX/MEM side-effect controls.\nnotes: \n--- hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 Split execute with operand latch\nSplit the current EX stage into a narrow EXA operand stage and an EXB execute stage. EXA resolves forwarding and latches post-forward rs1/rs2, immediate, control, pc, rd, and RVFI payload into a new operand register; EXB runs the existing fast RV32I ALU, branch compare/target selection, load/store address generation, and the held RV32M unit, then writes the existing EX/MEM boundary. Adjacent ALU dependencies can still bypass the EXB result into the next EXA latch, but the critical path becomes producer result to operand latch or operand latch through ALU to EX/MEM rather than forwarding muxes plus ALU plus branch/address control in one cycle. This should raise Fmax enough to cover the added branch and load-use latency, especially because CoreMark already tolerates the multi-cycle M unit and the failed predictor data suggests avoiding extra fetch-path complexity.\n\nlesson: - 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 (regression, +0.36%): Splitting EX into an EXA ope\n…[1828 of 4900 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c4e9e63560ec1138]…\nonly to choose the one-bit branch result. The ordinary ALU operand muxes remain for arithmetic, load/store address, JALR target, and RVFI operand reporting, but the timing-critical branch-taken-to-IF redirect path no longer needs to traverse two wide 3-way operand muxes before the equality or magnitude comparator.\n\nThis keeps the current metadata-free direct-JAL decode redirect, the two-entry IF FIFO, the single-cycle conditional branch resolution point, and all RVFI ordering semantics. It spends LUTs on duplicated branch compare logic rather than adding pipeline latency or front-end prediction state, aiming for a pure Fmax gain on the current u_id/u_ex/u_if critical cone with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n\nlesson: - 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r14s1 (placement_failed, n/a): A kept 3x3 branch compare matrix can exceed placement margin because duplicating 32-bit equality/signed/unsigned comparators fans each forwarding source into many wide compare cones instead of relieving the fragile EX redirect path.\nnotes: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
