{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r4s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-002-r4s1\ntitle: \"Decoupled two-entry instruction fetch queue\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The best current core is now limited by a modest execute path, while random\n  instruction-memory stalls still inject bubbles whenever io_imemReady is low.\n  CoreMark also has dmem stalls, load-use bubbles, redirects, and multi-cycle\n  RV32M operations that stop decode but do not inherently need to stop the\n  instruction bus from fetching future sequential instructions. A small\n  front-end queue should convert some of those backend stall cycles into useful\n  prefetch slack, improving iterations per cycle without putting branch\n  prediction, target adders, or tag lookups on the PC loop.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the purely combinational IF/ID boundary with a two-entry sequential\n  instruction fetch queue. The fetch side keeps issuing sequential PC values\n  while the queue has space and io_imemReady returns data; the decode side pops\n  one queued instruction only when the existing hazard logic says ID can accept\n  it. On load-use, dmem, and execute-busy stalls, the queue may continue filling\n  from imem. On an EX redirect, the queue is flushed and fetch restarts at the\n  redirect target, preserving the existing precise control-flow behavior.\n  Decode bubbles only when the queue is empty, so independent imem stalls can\n  be hidden after any earlier backend-created fill opportunity. This targets an\n  IPC gain from the 22% imem stall stream while avoiding the Fmax regressions\n  seen from predictor structures in the fetch PC recurrence.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 9\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Turn IF into a small valid/pc/instruction FIFO with separate fetch push and decode pop control, plus redirect flush handling.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Replace direct imem_ready bubbling with queue-empty/decode-accept control so backend stalls can hold decode while fetch may keep filling.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the fetch queue status and decode-pop handshake between IF, ID, and hazard control without changing the nret=1 top-level IO.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Capture queued IF payloads only when decode accepts an instruction, while preserving existing dmem hold and bubble semantics.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add any small front-end queue metadata fields needed to carry valid queued PC/instruction payloads cleanly.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
