ss path helps when dmem masks, misalignment checks, and RVFI aligned addresses otherwise inherit the full generic `alu_result` shift/compare/jump mux delay.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 (regression, +0.36%): Splitting EX into an EXA operand latch and EXB execute stage can fail to pay off when adjacent-result bypassing and hazard control still sit on the operand boundary while CoreMark pays the extra branch/load-use latency.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r3s2 (regression, -34.40%): Decode-side BTFN/JAL prediction still creates a long ID-to-IF PC-update feedback path through `imm` decode, target add, alignment, and stall/bubble gating, while EX `pred_next_pc` mismatch checks add control cost that CoreMark's saved taken-branch bubbles did not repay.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r4s0 (regression, +0.03%): Bypassing the generic ALU for ADD/ADDI/SUB/AUIPC did not match the load/store address win because duplicated EX carry chains, extra forwarded-operand fanout, and the fast-vs-generic `alu_result` mux outweighed removing ALU case-mux delay.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r4s1 (improvement, +5.76%): A two-entry IF FIFO helps CoreMark because load-use, dmem, and RV32M `ex_busy` holds let sequential fetch build slack that hides later `io_imemReady` gaps without adding predictor or target-adder delay to the PC loop.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r4s2 (regression, -28.77%): Even a one-entry EX-trained last-target predictor regressed because the IF `pc` path still gained a tag compare, target mux, predictor update/invalidate state, and carried `pred_pc` mismatch plumbing that CoreMark's hot backedges could not repay on this timing point.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
