{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r5s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-002-r5s1\ntitle: \"Four-entry fetch skid queue\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion already proves that a tiny instruction fetch queue can improve CoreMark by using load-use, dmem, and RV32M busy cycles to prefetch through the randomized imem stall stream. The remaining worst path now ends in the IF queue count state, so simply adding predictor logic or target calculation is risky, but increasing sequential buffering without new control-flow speculation may still improve IPC enough to overcome a small timing cost.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the hand-coded two-entry IF FIFO with a shallow four-entry circular skid queue built from small arrays, head/tail pointers, and a compact count. The fetch side should keep issuing sequential PCs whenever the queue is not full, including cycles where decode is held by dmem, load-use, or mul/div busy; the decode side should pop exactly one instruction only on the existing hazard-unit accept handshake. EX redirects flush the queue and restart fetch at the resolved target. This is a structural front-end depth change, not a predictor: it preserves the current PC+4 fetch recurrence and avoids target adders, tags, or branch metadata. CoreMark sees both 22 percent imem stalls and frequent backend bubbles, so doubling the amount of prefetched sequential work should hide more independent imem gaps than the two-entry queue can, with modest LUT/FF cost on the Tang Nano 20K.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Replace the two-entry special-case FIFO with a four-entry circular instruction queue that supports simultaneous push/pop and redirect flush.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Keep the existing decode-pop contract, but treat the queue valid/empty indication as the sole instruction-availability signal so backend holds can continue to build fetch slack.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the unchanged decode-pop and IF/ID payload interface to the deeper fetch queue without altering the nret=1 top-level IO contract.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
