imm` address path helps when dmem masks, misalignment checks, and RVFI aligned addresses otherwise inherit the full generic `alu_result` shift/compare/jump mux delay.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 (regression, +0.36%): Spl\n…[1425 of 4497 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c9b6a207de8384c7]…\nthis timing point.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r5s0 (regression, -3.99%): In the two-entry IF FIFO, dropping `redirect` from `bypass_valid`/`out_valid`/`do_push` regressed because the count update still depends on the redirect flush and hazard-generated `decode_pop`, so local handshake slimming can perturb synthesis without removing the real EX-redirect-to-count arc.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r5s1 (regression, -0.42%): Deepening the IF FIFO from two entries to a four-entry circular queue did not repay its head/tail/indexed-read/count logic, suggesting CoreMark's useful sequential prefetch is already mostly captured before extra IF queue state becomes the timing constraint.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r5s2 (regression, -15.27%): Even a 4-entry return-address stack that avoids front-end immediate decode can regress when canonical-JALR recognition and `ret_redirect`/`ret_pred_valid` repair plumbing create a new ID-to-IF/EX control path; CoreMark's return savings were too sparse to repay the timing cost.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
