{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r3s1\\\") | .hypothesis.hypothesis, .implementation_notes, .lesson, (.critical_path|tostring)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r3s1\\\") | .hypothesis.hypothesis, .implementation_notes, .lesson, (.critical_path|tostring)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"Split the current EX stage into a narrow EXA operand stage and an EXB execute stage. EXA resolves forwarding and latches post-forward rs1/rs2, immediate, control, pc, rd, and RVFI payload into a new operand register; EXB runs the existing fast RV32I ALU, branch compare/target selection, load/store address generation, and the held RV32M unit, then writes the existing EX/MEM boundary. Adjacent ALU dependencies can still bypass the EXB result into the next EXA latch, but the critical path becomes producer result to operand latch or operand latch through ALU to EX/MEM rather than forwarding muxes plus ALU plus branch/address control in one cycle. This should raise Fmax enough to cover the added branch and load-use latency, especially because CoreMark already tolerates the multi-cycle M unit and the failed predictor data suggests avoiding extra fetch-path complexity.\n\n\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 (regression, +0.36%): Splitting EX into an EXA operand latch and EXB execute stage can fail to pay off when adjacent-result bypassing and hazard control still sit on the operand boundary while CoreMark pays the extra branch/load-use latency.\n{\"from\":\"cpu/u_ex/exa_q.rs2_val_0_s0\",\"to\":\"cpu/u_ex/u_muldiv/mul_ss_63_s0\",\"slack_ns\":-7.965,\"arrival_ns\":18.039,\"logic_cells\":38,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_if\",\"cpu/u_ex\"]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r2s1\\\") | .hypothesis.hypothesis, .implementation_notes, .lesson, (.critical_path|tostring)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"Retiming control-flow squash should improve Fmax with little or no CoreMark\nIPC loss. Let EX redirect drive only the PC update in the redirect cycle, and\nallow the younger wrong-path instruction to be captured into ID/EX. A small\nregistered \"kill younger\" control in EX then converts that instruction into a\nbubble before it can update EX/MEM, dmem, regfile, or RVFI state. Load-use,\ndmem, imem, and mul/div busy stalls keep their existing replay behavior, but\nredirect no longer feeds hazard_unit flush_id/flush_if or the IF output NOP\nmux. This breaks the EX-to-hazard-to-ID feedback path while preserving the\nexisting branch recovery latency and the nret=1 RVFI contract.\n\n\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (regression, +21.59%): Retiming redirect squash into EX can regress when the saved EX-to-hazard flush arc is replaced by high-fanout `squash_id_ex`/`execute_valid` gating across branch/jump, RV32M start, trap, and EX/MEM side-effect controls.\n{\"from\":\"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s\",\"to\":\"dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s\",\"slack_ns\":-12.773,\"arrival_ns\":23.068,\"logic_cells\":23,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_id\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\",\"cpu/u_ex\"]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r4s1\\\") | .hypothesis.hypothesis, .implementation_notes, .lesson, (.critical_path|tostring)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r4s1\\\") | .hypothesis.hypothesis, .implementation_notes, .lesson, (.critical_path|tostring)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"Replace the purely combinational IF/ID boundary with a two-entry sequential\ninstruction fetch queue. The fetch side keeps issuing sequential PC values\nwhile the queue has space and io_imemReady returns data; the decode side pops\none queued instruction only when the existing hazard logic says ID can accept\nit. On load-use, dmem, and execute-busy stalls, the queue may continue filling\nfrom imem. On an EX redirect, the queue is flushed and fetch restarts at the\nredirect target, preserving the existing precise control-flow behavior.\nDecode bubbles only when the queue is empty, so independent imem stalls can\nbe hidden after any earlier backend-created fill opportunity. This targets an\nIPC gain from the 22% imem stall stream while avoiding the Fmax regressions\nseen from predictor structures in the fetch PC recurrence.\n\n\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r4s1 (improvement, +5.76%): A two-entry IF FIFO helps CoreMark because load-use, dmem, and RV32M `ex_busy` holds let sequential fetch build slack that hides later `io_imemReady` gaps without adding predictor or target-adder delay to the PC loop.\n{\"from\":\"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0\",\"to\":\"cpu/u_if/count_q_0_s1\",\"slack_ns\":-10.894,\"arrival_ns\":21.188,\"logic_cells\":27,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_id\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_if\"]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
