{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id|test(\\\"hyp-20261003-(002-r2s1|002-r3s1|002-r7s1)\\\")) | \\\"ID: \\\\(.id)\\\\nTITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nOUTCOME: \\\\(.outcome) delta=\\\\(.delta_pct//\\\"n/a\\\") fmax=\\\\(.fmax_mhz//\\\"n/a\\\") levels=\\\\(.logic_levels//\\\"n/a\\\")\\\\nHYP: \\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nLESSON: \\\\(.lesson//\\\"\\\")\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "ID: hyp-20261003-002-r2s1\nTITLE: Retimed redirect squash\nOUTCOME: regression delta=21.59 fmax=58.792 levels=16\nHYP: Retiming control-flow squash should improve Fmax with little or no CoreMark\nIPC loss. Let EX redirect drive only the PC update in the redirect cycle, and\nallow the younger wrong-path instruction to be captured into ID/EX. A small\nregistered \"kill younger\" control in EX then converts that instruction into a\nbubble before it can update EX/MEM, dmem, regfile, or RVFI state. Load-use,\ndmem, imem, and mul/div busy stalls keep their existing replay behavior, but\nredirect no longer feeds hazard_unit flush_id/flush_if or the IF output NOP\nmux. This breaks the EX-to-hazard-to-ID feedback path while preserving the\nexisting branch recovery latency and the nret=1 RVFI contract.\n\nLESSON: - 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (regression, +21.59%): Retiming redirect squash into EX can regress when the saved EX-to-hazard flush arc is replaced by high-fanout `squash_id_ex`/`execute_valid` gating across branch/jump, RV32M start, trap, and EX/MEM side-effect controls.\n\nID: hyp-20261003-002-r3s1\nTITLE: Split execute with operand latch\nOUTCOME: regression delta=0.36 fmax=74.789 levels=13\nHYP: Split the current EX stage into a narrow EXA operand stage and an EXB execute stage. EXA resolves forwarding and latches post-forward rs1/rs2, immediate, control, pc, rd, and RVFI payload into a new operand register; EXB runs the existing fast RV32I ALU, branch compare/target selection, load/store address generation, and the held RV32M unit, then writes the existing EX/MEM boundary. Adjacent ALU dependencies can still bypass the EXB result into the next EXA latch, but the critical path becomes producer result to operand latch or operand latch through ALU to EX/MEM rather than forwarding muxes plus ALU plus branch/address control in one cycle. This should raise Fmax enough to cover the added branch and load-use latency, especially because CoreMark already tolerates the multi-cycle M unit and the failed predictor data suggests avoiding extra fetch-path complexity.\n\nLESSON: - 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 (regression, +0.36%): Splitting EX into an EXA operand latch and EXB execute stage can fail to pay off when adjacent-result bypassing and hazard control still sit on the operand boundary while CoreMark pays the extra branch/load-use latency.\n\nID: hyp-20261003-002-r7s1\nTITLE: One-entry nonblocking store buffer\nOUTCOME: placement_failed delta=n/a fmax=n/a levels=n/a\nHYP: Add a one-entry store buffer at the MEM/dmem boundary for ordinary RAM stores.\nWhen a store reaches MEM and dmem is not ready, latch its aligned address,\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI memory metadata into the buffer,\nallow the store instruction to retire in order, and let younger non-memory\ninstructions continue while the buffer retries the external write. The buffer\nowns the dmem port until the write is accepted; a younger load or store stalls\nbehind it, preserving memory ordering. MMIO and marker-range stores remain\nblocking and are never buffered, so CoreMark timing brackets and UART behavior\nstay exact.\n\nThis is a structural decoupling change rather than another front-end tweak:\nloads still observe the existing ready/valid rule, RVFI order remains single\nretire on channel 0, and the two-entry IF FIFO is left alone. The expected win\nis IPC from overlapping random store wait cycles with later integer work, with\nonly a small Fmax cost from a local dmem-output mux and a memory-op stall\ncondition.\n\nLESSON: - 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r7s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Even a one-entry nonblocking store buffer can exceed the bench core's placement margin because it adds a second dmem owner, MEM-stage output muxing, and global `mem_block` stall control around the already-fragile memory boundary.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
