{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r10s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-003-r10s2\ntitle: \"Countdown loop-back predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Prior predictor attempts on this core lost because they put target adders,\n  tag compares, return-stack plumbing, or prediction-repair compares on the\n  fragile IF/redirect timing boundary. CoreMark still has hot taken loop\n  backedges, so the promising predictor angle is to predict only a repeated\n  loop branch without decoding branch immediates or doing a BTB lookup in IF.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a single-entry loop-back predictor trained from the already-registered\n  EX/MEM payload of taken conditional branches. When a backward, word-aligned\n  branch retires through EX/MEM, record its target, instruction word, and the\n  sequential fetch distance from target to branch PC. The IF stage then counts\n  accepted sequential fetches after returning to the target; when the countdown\n  reaches the recorded branch slot and the fetched instruction word matches,\n  it predicts the branch taken by setting the next fetch PC to the recorded\n  target and tags that fetched branch with a one-bit predicted-taken marker.\n\n  EX still computes the real branch condition and target. If the tagged branch\n  is taken, EX suppresses the duplicate redirect; if it falls through, EX\n  redirects to pc+4 to repair the loop exit. Because the predictor uses a\n  countdown plus instruction match rather than a fetch-time immediate decoder,\n  BTB tag array, or full predicted-next-PC compare, it should recover the hot\n  loop redirect bubbles that CoreMark repeatedly pays while adding only a small\n  counter, target mux, and one metadata bit to the existing two-entry IF queue.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add a one-bit predicted-taken field to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles so a loop prediction can be repaired or confirmed in EX.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add the single-entry EX/MEM-trained countdown loop predictor, per-entry predicted-taken queue bits, and conservative invalidation on nonmatching redirects or instruction mismatch.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Latch the predicted-taken metadata through ID/EX alongside the existing instruction payload without changing decode legality rules.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Use the predicted-taken bit to suppress duplicate redirects for correctly predicted taken branches and redirect to pc+4 on predicted-taken loop exits.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire registered branch-training information from EX/MEM back to IF and carry the one-bit prediction metadata through the single-retire pipeline.\"\n  - file: test/test_loop_predictor.py\n    description: \"Add focused tests for repeated taken loop prediction, loop-exit repair, instruction mismatch invalidation, and unchanged behavior for unpredicted branches.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
