{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-003-r7s2\\\") | {id,title,motivation:.motivation,hypothesis:.hypothesis.hypothesis,expected_impact,changes,outcome,fitness,delta_pct,fmax_mhz,cycles,lut4,ff,critical_path,implementation_notes} | @json' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"{\"id\":\"hyp-20261003-003-r7s2\",\"title\":\"ID-stage direct-JAL predictor\",\"motivation\":\"The predictor experiments so far show a consistent failure mode: anything\\nthat adds BTB tags, counters, return-stack state, conditional-branch\\nimmediates, or broad predicted-next-PC repair logic to the fetch feedback\\npath costs more Fmax than CoreMark gains in IPC. Direct JALs are the\\nnarrow remaining control-flow case worth isolating: they are always taken,\\ntheir target uses the existing decoded immediate, and they do not need a\\ndirection predictor or speculation recovery.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Add a zero-table static predictor for direct JAL only at the decode\\nboundary. When the IF/ID instruction is a valid JAL that decode will accept\\nthis cycle, compute the aligned target as pc plus the existing J-immediate\\nand redirect the IF stage immediately, flushing only younger queued fetch\\nentries. Carry a single \\\"direct JAL already redirected\\\" bit into ID/EX so\\nEX still produces the architectural pc_next and rd=pc+4 value, but\\nsuppresses its normal later redirect for that same JAL. Misaligned JAL\\ntargets, JALR, and all conditional branches keep the existing EX-resolution\\nbehavior.\\n\\nThis should recover one redirect cycle for calls and direct jumps without\\nreintroducing the failed ingredients from earlier predictor attempts: no\\nBTB lookup, no branch-direction table, no RAS, no conditional branch target\\npredecode, and no full predicted-next-PC mismatch compare. The risk is the\\nnew ID-to-IF target-adder path, but limiting it to accepted direct JALs\\nshould keep the timing cost much smaller than previous predictor designs.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":5,\"confidence\":\"low\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a one-bit ID/EX flag marking direct JAL instructions whose fetch redirect was already performed in decode.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Recognize accepted direct JALs, reuse the decoded J-immediate to form an aligned early target, and latch the already-redirected flag.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Accept a decode-stage direct-JAL redirect that clears younger queued entries and restarts fetch at the supplied target without changing the two-entry FIFO policy.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Suppress the normal EX redirect for direct JALs that were already redirected in decode while preserving RVFI pc_next and link-register writeback.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Wire the decode-stage direct-JAL redirect and the ID/EX already-redirected metadata through the existing nret=1 core.\"}],\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"fitness\":150.01,\"delta_pct\":3.96,\"fmax_mhz\":66.097,\"cycles\":4406210,\"lut4\":3046,\"ff\":839,\"critical_path\":{\"from\":\"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0\",\"to\":\"cpu/u_ex/u_muldiv/result_q_0_s0\",\"slack_ns\":-11.128,\"arrival_ns\":21.423,\"logic_cells\":10,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_id\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_ex/u_muldiv\"]},\"implementation_notes\":\"\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
