{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'rg -n \"category: predictor|predictor|BTB|BTFN|JAL|branch|return|RAS|last-target|last-taken|countdown|loop\" cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261003-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move RV32M onto a held multi-cycle execute unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current vendor timing path starts in pipeline state, flows through the\\nforwarding network into EX, crosses the monolithic ALU, and ends near the\\nmemory-side outputs. Because the ALU contains fully combinational multiply,\\ndivide, and remainder hardware, every simple ADD, branch compare, and address\\ncalculation pays for deep RV32M arithmetic in the timed EX cone even when the\\ninstruction is not an M-extension op.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current vendor timing path starts in pipeline state, flows through the\\nforwarding network into EX, crosses the monolithic ALU, and ends near the\\nmemory-side outputs. Because the ALU contains fully combinational multiply,\\ndivide, and remainder hardware, every simple ADD, branch compare, and address\\ncalculation pays for deep RV32M arithmetic in the timed EX cone even when the\\ninstruction is not an M-extension op.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the execute stage into a fast single-cycle integer/branch/address path\\nand a held multi-cycle RV32M substage. MUL/MULH can use a short registered DSP\\npath, while DIV/REM use an iterative divider/remainder sequencer that stalls\\nIF/ID/EX until the result is ready. CoreMark should lose a few cycles on the\\nrelatively infrequent M operations, but the common ALU, branch, and load/store\\naddress path should retime to a much higher Fmax, improving the fitness product.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 22, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add execute-stage state