{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r7s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-003-r7s2\ntitle: \"ID-stage direct-JAL predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The predictor experiments so far show a consistent failure mode: anything\n  that adds BTB tags, counters, return-stack state, conditional-branch\n  immediates, or broad predicted-next-PC repair logic to the fetch feedback\n  path costs more Fmax than CoreMark gains in IPC. Direct JALs are the\n  narrow remaining control-flow case worth isolating: they are always taken,\n  their target uses the existing decoded immediate, and they do not need a\n  direction predictor or speculation recovery.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a zero-table static predictor for direct JAL only at the decode\n  boundary. When the IF/ID instruction is a valid JAL that decode will accept\n  this cycle, compute the aligned target as pc plus the existing J-immediate\n  and redirect the IF stage immediately, flushing only younger queued fetch\n  entries. Carry a single \"direct JAL already redirected\" bit into ID/EX so\n  EX still produces the architectural pc_next and rd=pc+4 value, but\n  suppresses its normal later redirect for that same JAL. Misaligned JAL\n  targets, JALR, and all conditional branches keep the existing EX-resolution\n  behavior.\n\n  This should recover one redirect cycle for calls and direct jumps without\n  reintroducing the failed ingredients from earlier predictor attempts: no\n  BTB lookup, no branch-direction table, no RAS, no conditional branch target\n  predecode, and no full predicted-next-PC mismatch compare. The risk is the\n  new ID-to-IF target-adder path, but limiting it to accepted direct JALs\n  should keep the timing cost much smaller than previous predictor designs.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add a one-bit ID/EX flag marking direct JAL instructions whose fetch redirect was already performed in decode.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Recognize accepted direct JALs, reuse the decoded J-immediate to form an aligned early target, and latch the already-redirected flag.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Accept a decode-stage direct-JAL redirect that clears younger queued entries and restarts fetch at the supplied target without changing the two-entry FIFO policy.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Suppress the normal EX redirect for direct JALs that were already redirected in decode while preserving RVFI pc_next and link-register writeback.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the decode-stage direct-JAL redirect and the ID/EX already-redirected metadata through the existing nret=1 core.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
